lton","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ernest Rutherford","is_correct":true}],"explanation":"Rutherford’s interpretation of scattering experiments established the nuclear atom.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 34","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":653,"stem":"Who is credited with discovering the atomic nucleus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"J. J. Thomson","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"James Chadwick","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"John Dalton","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ernest Rutherford","is_correct":true}],"explanation":"Rutherford’s interpretation of scattering experiments established the nuclear atom.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 34","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"ab1ff7d1-09a6-49b6-863d-199c861b6fa9","subject_id":"a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001"}},{"entry":654,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"194bf0a8-6ebf-41d3-ab24-3733c15d686a","source":{"entry":654,"stem":"What is the chemical formula of common table salt?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NaCl","is_correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"KCl","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NaOH","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CaCO₃","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Table salt is sodium chloride, NaCl.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 35","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":654,"stem":"What is the chemical formula of common table salt?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NaCl","is_correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"KCl","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NaOH","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CaCO₃","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Table salt is sodium chloride, NaCl.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 35","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"194bf0a8-6ebf-41d3-ab24-3733c15d686a","subject_id":"a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001"}},{"entry":655,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"3f35c750-6538-4d83-8020-709cb82065b5","source":{"entry":655,"stem":"Ordinary chemical matter is composed of atoms and combinations of what?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only photons","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atoms such as molecules and ions","is_correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Only free neutrons","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Only sound waves","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Atoms combine into molecules or form ions in ordinary chemical substances.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 36","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":655,"stem":"Ordinary chemical matter is composed of atoms and combinations of what?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only photons","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atoms such as molecules and ions","is_correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Only free neutrons","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Only sound waves","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Atoms combine into molecules or form ions in ordinary chemical substances.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 36","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"3f35c750-6538-4d83-8020-709cb82065b5","subject_id":"a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001"}},{"entry":656,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"4b714640-8574-494c-951d-23f7aa45ae91","source":{"entry":656,"stem":"At high temperature, nitrogen and oxygen can react directly to form which oxide initially?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrous oxide, N₂O","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dinitrogen pentoxide, N₂O₅","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitric oxide, NO","is_correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrogen trioxide, NO₃","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"The high-temperature reaction is N₂ + O₂ → 2NO.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 37","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":656,"stem":"At high temperature, nitrogen and oxygen can react directly to form which oxide initially?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrous oxide, N₂O","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dinitrogen pentoxide, N₂O₅","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitric oxide, NO","is_correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrogen trioxide, NO₃","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"The high-temperature reaction is N₂ + O₂ → 2NO.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 37","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"4b714640-8574-494c-951d-23f7aa45ae91","subject_id":"a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001"}},{"entry":657,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"bc54cb40-164b-4940-8c3b-845d33e5d067","source":{"entry":657,"stem":"What defines an adiabatic process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Constant temperature in all cases","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No work done in all cases","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Constant pressure in all cases","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No heat transfer across the system boundary","is_correct":true}],"explanation":"An adiabatic process has Q = 0, although work can still transfer energy.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 38","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":657,"stem":"What defines an adiabatic process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Constant temperature in all cases","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No work done in all cases","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Constant pressure in all cases","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No heat transfer across the system boundary","is_correct":true}],"explanation":"An adiabatic process has Q = 0, although work can still transfer energy.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 38","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"bc54cb40-164b-4940-8c3b-845d33e5d067","subject_id":"439d7f25-5f12-49ff-84f6-6d161b79a4a9"}},{"entry":658,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"d8a52b41-7d13-40c6-aa4c-9f64cfd0f434","source":{"entry":658,"stem":"Which equation converts Celsius temperature C to Fahrenheit temperature F?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"F = (9/5)C + 32","is_correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"F = (5/9)C + 32","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"F = C + 273.15","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"F = (9/5)(C − 32)","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Multiply Celsius by 9/5 and then add 32.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 39","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":658,"stem":"Which equation converts Celsius temperature C to Fahrenheit temperature F?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"F = (9/5)C + 32","is_correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"F = (5/9)C + 32","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"F = C + 273.15","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"F = (9/5)(C − 32)","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Multiply Celsius by 9/5 and then add 32.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 39","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"d8a52b41-7d13-40c6-aa4c-9f64cfd0f434","subject_id":"439d7f25-5f12-49ff-84f6-6d161b79a4a9"}},{"entry":659,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"ba77de81-6b40-44ef-af57-c51a5fd8309c","source":{"entry":659,"stem":"What is the conventional unit of optical power of a lens?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metre","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dioptre","is_correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Candela","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lumen","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Lens power is reciprocal focal length in metres, measured in dioptres.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 40","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":659,"stem":"What is the conventional unit of optical power of a lens?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metre","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dioptre","is_correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Candela","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lumen","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Lens power is reciprocal focal length in metres, measured in dioptres.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 40","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"ba77de81-6b40-44ef-af57-c51a5fd8309c","subject_id":"439d7f25-5f12-49ff-84f6-6d161b79a4a9"}},{"entry":660,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"1e108e0e-1fd0-4372-97e6-fab88ec94334","source":{"entry":660,"stem":"What is the SI unit of mechanical stress?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Newton","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Joule","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pascal","is_correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Watt","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Stress is force per area, measured in N/m² or pascals.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 41","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":660,"stem":"What is the SI unit of mechanical stress?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Newton","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Joule","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pascal","is_correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Watt","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Stress is force per area, measured in N/m² or pascals.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 41","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"1e108e0e-1fd0-4372-97e6-fab88ec94334","subject_id":"439d7f25-5f12-49ff-84f6-6d161b79a4a9"}},{"entry":661,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"3650aad8-d3d9-4be4-9e59-8b349b7e3f11","source":{"entry":661,"stem":"Which weak acid forms when carbon dioxide dissolves in rainwater?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sulfuric acid, H₂SO₄","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrochloric acid, HCl","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitric acid, HNO₃","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbonic acid, H₂CO₃","is_correct":true}],"explanation":"Dissolved CO₂ reacts with water to form carbonic acid.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 42","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":661,"stem":"Which weak acid forms when carbon dioxide dissolves in rainwater?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sulfuric acid, H₂SO₄","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrochloric acid, HCl","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitric acid, HNO₃","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbonic acid, H₂CO₃","is_correct":true}],"explanation":"Dissolved CO₂ reacts with water to form carbonic acid.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 42","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"3650aad8-d3d9-4be4-9e59-8b349b7e3f11","subject_id":"a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001"}},{"entry":662,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"6ddf68dd-cdf6-4b82-81b3-c181f8ea9a17","source":{"entry":662,"stem":"What is the SI unit of power?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Watt","is_correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Joule","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Newton","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coulomb","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"One watt is one joule of energy transferred per second.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 43","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":662,"stem":"What is the SI unit of power?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Watt","is_correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Joule","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Newton","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coulomb","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"One watt is one joule of energy transferred per second.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 43","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"6ddf68dd-cdf6-4b82-81b3-c181f8ea9a17","subject_id":"439d7f25-5f12-49ff-84f6-6d161b79a4a9"}},{"entry":663,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"65ee692f-852e-4e6c-9c60-a441365bad68","source":{"entry":663,"stem":"Among alpha, beta and gamma radiation, which generally has the greatest penetration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha radiation","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gamma radiation","is_correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Beta radiation","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All have equal penetration","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Gamma photons generally penetrate matter more deeply than alpha or beta particles.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 44","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":663,"stem":"Among alpha, beta and gamma radiation, which generally has the greatest penetration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha radiation","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gamma radiation","is_correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Beta radiation","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All have equal penetration","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Gamma photons generally penetrate matter more deeply than alpha or beta particles.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 44","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"65ee692f-852e-4e6c-9c60-a441365bad68","subject_id":"36b65a9a-2171-424e-99e2-e41e08d259cc"}},{"entry":664,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"b0dc1375-0fb0-4bc3-84fe-92015b79c46a","source":{"entry":664,"stem":"Which products form when sodium hydroxide fully neutralises sulfuric acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium chloride and hydrogen","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium nitrate and oxygen","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium sulfate and water","is_correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium carbonate and methane","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"2NaOH + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 45","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":664,"stem":"Which products form when sodium hydroxide fully neutralises sulfuric acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium chloride and hydrogen","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium nitrate and oxygen","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium sulfate and water","is_correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium carbonate and methane","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"2NaOH + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 45","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"b0dc1375-0fb0-4bc3-84fe-92015b79c46a","subject_id":"a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001"}},{"entry":665,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"496784b4-fcbe-4860-9e8a-d638e04b2245","source":{"entry":665,"stem":"What is the molecular formula of glucose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CH₃COOH","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C₂H₅OH","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C₆H₁₂O₆","is_correct":true}],"explanation":"Glucose contains six carbon, twelve hydrogen and six oxygen atoms.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 46","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":665,"stem":"What is the molecular formula of glucose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CH₃COOH","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C₂H₅OH","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C₆H₁₂O₆","is_correct":true}],"explanation":"Glucose contains six carbon, twelve hydrogen and six oxygen atoms.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 46","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"496784b4-fcbe-4860-9e8a-d638e04b2245","subject_id":"a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001"}},{"entry":666,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"e2080d7d-b7ba-4aa0-b4fb-d4a380981b6b","source":{"entry":666,"stem":"Newton’s first law is also known as which law?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Law of inertia","is_correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Law of gravitation","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Law of conservation of charge","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ohm’s law","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"The first law describes the persistence of rest or uniform straight-line motion without net force.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 47","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":666,"stem":"Newton’s first law is also known as which law?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Law of inertia","is_correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Law of gravitation","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Law of conservation of charge","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ohm’s law","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"The first law describes the persistence of rest or uniform straight-line motion without net force.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 47","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"e2080d7d-b7ba-4aa0-b4fb-d4a380981b6b","subject_id":"83e951c5-9e30-47c7-8848-a7c7f9abb484"}},{"entry":667,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"f58100a2-bc2a-4672-833f-584d26a08203","source":{"entry":667,"stem":"What is the ability of the same substance to exist in different crystal structures called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isomorphism","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polymorphism","is_correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ionisation","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sublimation","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Polymorphism means multiple crystalline forms of the same substance.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 48","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":667,"stem":"What is the ability of the same substance to exist in different crystal structures called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isomorphism","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polymorphism","is_correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ionisation","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sublimation","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"Polymorphism means multiple crystalline forms of the same substance.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 48","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"f58100a2-bc2a-4672-833f-584d26a08203","subject_id":"a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001"}},{"entry":668,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"31787056-cb6a-4541-9821-e50b0f9031c8","source":{"entry":668,"stem":"When an object is between a concave mirror’s pole and focus, what image forms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Real, inverted and diminished","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Real, upright and equal-sized","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Virtual, upright and magnified","is_correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Virtual, inverted and diminished","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"A concave mirror gives an upright magnified virtual image for an object within its focal length.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 49","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":668,"stem":"When an object is between a concave mirror’s pole and focus, what image forms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Real, inverted and diminished","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Real, upright and equal-sized","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Virtual, upright and magnified","is_correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Virtual, inverted and diminished","is_correct":false}],"explanation":"A concave mirror gives an upright magnified virtual image for an object within its focal length.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 49","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[],"id":"31787056-cb6a-4541-9821-e50b0f9031c8","subject_id":"36b65a9a-2171-424e-99e2-e41e08d259cc"}},{"entry":669,"status":"INSERT","question_id":"74522b87-87b4-4c8e-8078-209b27bc3664","source":{"entry":669,"stem":"Which element has the highest electronegativity on the Pauling scale?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxygen","is_correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrogen","is_correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorine","is_correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fluorine","is_correct":true}],"explanation":"Fluorine has the highest Pauling electronegativity.","source":"File 2 Science, printed question 50","editorial_status":null,"provenance":null,"references":[]},"record":{"entry":66